// `se.kin.QuatLeakyIntegrator` against the closed form its leak law implies.
//
// Drive it with a CONSTANT body rate about a fixed axis and the SO(3) problem
// collapses to a scalar one, exactly: `log q` starts at zero, so it starts
// parallel to `w`, and the bracket `w - wc*log q` keeps it parallel forever.
// Everything then lives in the plane spanned by 1 and the axis, and the angle
// obeys
//
//     theta_dot = |w| - wc * theta          =>   theta(t) = (|w|/wc)(1 - e^-wc*t)
//
// with `q(t) = (cos(theta/2), sin(theta/2) * axis)`. That is a real closed form
// for the whole block -- the log map, the leak, the Hamilton product and the
// integration -- and not a restatement of any of them.
//
// |w| = 0.3 rad/s, wc = 0.2 rad/s, so theta rises to 1.5 rad and reaches 1.297
// by t = 10 s. Both numbers are chosen: the steady state stays well under pi so
// the shortest-rotation flip never engages and the principal branch is the
// whole story, and 10 s is two time constants, enough that the exponential is
// carrying the answer rather than the initial slope.
//
// WITHOUT the leak this column is theta = |w|t = 3 rad at t = 10 against
// 1.297, so a dropped or mis-signed leak term is not a tolerance question.
//
// The axis is (1,2,2)/3 rather than a coordinate direction, so all four
// components move and are mutually distinct: a transposed axis in the Hamilton
// product, or a leak applied to the wrong component, moves a column.
package qint;

use se.math;
use se.kin;

node QLeak {
    outputs { q: kin.Quat; }

    structure {
        node wv : math.Merge3(rad/s) {};
        node qi : kin.QuatLeakyIntegrator { wc = 0.2 (rad/s); };

        0.1 --> wv.x;
        0.2 --> wv.y, wv.z;

        wv --> qi;
        qi.q --> self;
    }
}
